codex
The new pre-commit design-refs gate can miss exactly the staged commits it is intended to guard, especially deletions and partial commits with unstaged coverage changes. These are correctness gaps in the new hook behavior.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Include staged design deletions in the trigger — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/dh3-remainder/scripts/check-design-refs.sh:137-139
  When `--staged-only` is used from pre-commit, `--diff-filter=ACMR` excludes deleted files, so a commit that removes the only `dev/design/...` document mentioning a requirement token exits here without running the coverage sweep. That lets the commit drop an existing design-of-record link even though the hook is meant to fire when design coverage moves; include deletions for the trigger even if later readers ignore missing files.

- [P2] Validate the staged snapshot, not the worktree — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/dh3-remainder/scripts/check-design-refs.sh:256-258
  In pre-commit mode this still invokes `commission-manifest.sh` against the current working tree, not the index. With a partial commit, a staged release-state entry can be accepted because an unstaged design back-link exists in the worktree, leaving the actual commit with no design coverage; the reverse can also block a clean staged commit because of unrelated unstaged edits. The hook needs to run against a staged snapshot or read files via the index when `--staged-only` is set.
The new pre-commit design-refs gate can miss exactly the staged commits it is intended to guard, especially deletions and partial commits with unstaged coverage changes. These are correctness gaps in the new hook behavior.

